Texas Instruments' product-change-notification page, updated March 17, 2026, says notifiable changes are communicated with the affected products, expected impact, qualification plan or results, sample availability and projected production shipment date. TI also says custom-device changes require customer approval. The mechanism matters more than the specific company: downstream users need time and evidence to decide whether a supplier's change preserves form, fit, function, quality and reliability.

TSMC describes qualification before a new technology transfers to manufacturing, manufacturing defense systems linked through its execution system and abnormal-signal escalation. Those statements describe a quality architecture, not proof that every individual change is riskless. The value is disciplined traceability and containment when reality differs from the plan.

The economic paradox

Freezing a process can look conservative because it delays a promising improvement. But an uncontrolled change can create scrap, customer requalification, field failures and weeks of root-cause work across many future lots.

The right comparison is therefore not “change versus no change.” It is uncontrolled learning versus controlled learning.

A strong change system can improve faster because it protects a reference point, makes experimental evidence interpretable and lets successful revisions scale with confidence. The biggest takeaway is simple: manufacturing progress depends on preserving enough stability to know whether a change actually worked.

